 "method": "People with income in the past 12 months below the poverty level (B17001_002) divided by the population for whom poverty status is determined (B17001_001), times 100. That universe excludes people in institutions, military group quarters and college dormitories, and unrelated children under 15. Margins of error derived with the Census Bureau's formula for proportions. Citywide figures are for New York city (place 3651000); peers are matched on place GEOID (state + place FIPS) from data/peers.json; borough figures are the five counties.",
 "caveats": [
  "The federal poverty thresholds are the same nationwide and do not reflect New York's housing costs; see the separate Poverty Tracker measure for a cost-adjusted rate.",
  "Race groups B (Black) and D (Asian) are race alone and include Hispanic members of those races; H is white alone, not Hispanic; I is Hispanic or Latino of any race.",
  "The ACS 1-year 2020 standard tables were never released (the Census Bureau published only experimental estimates because of pandemic data-collection problems), so 2020 is missing from every ACS series.",
  "The 2005 ACS covered only people in households; group quarters (dorms, prisons, nursing homes, shelters) were added in 2006. 2005 values are not strictly comparable to later years.",
  "All ACS figures are survey estimates. moe is the 90 percent margin of error, published by the Census Bureau for single cells and derived with the Bureau's approximation formulas for sums, shares and ratios.",
  "Margins of error: for 2010, 2011, 2012, 2013, 2014, 2015, 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025 the moe is the one the Census Bureau publishes in subject table S1701 (which matches this estimate to its published rounding) for the city, the 2025 peers and the boroughs. Other years, and the race breakdowns, use the derived margin described in the method."
 ]
}
